# LinkedIn Post Templates — Design & Authoring Guide

These are the dev-only LinkedIn post composer templates rendered at
`/posts` in development. Each post is a single React component that fills
an exact **1080 × 1350 px** canvas (LinkedIn 4:5 portrait).

## Canvas

- **Exact pixel size:** 1080 × 1350 (do not change — `SocialPostsPage`
  scales the canvas via `transform: scale()` to fit the viewport, so
  internal layouts must assume the real pixel grid)
- **Content padding:** 80 px on all sides (already applied by
  `.social-canvas__content` in `SocialPosts.css` — do not double up)
- **Variants:** `light` (white background, dark text), `dark` (gunmetal,
  white text), `accent` (gradient, white text). Register the variant in
  the `POSTS` array — never set background colour manually on the canvas.

## Typography minimums (mandatory)

LinkedIn down-samples images and most users view on mobile. Type that
looks fine in DevTools at 100% can become unreadable in the feed. Use
these floors:

| Element | Minimum px | Recommended | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Hero / pull quote | 72 | **78–90** | `font-heading`, medium or light |
| Section heading | 28 | 32–36 | tight tracking, semibold |
| Body / card body | **20** | 22–24 | never below 18 px |
| Card title | 24 | **26–28** | semibold |
| Stat value | 36 | **40–48** | font-heading, semibold |
| Footer / metadata | 18 | 20–22 | medium weight |
| Uppercase + letter-spacing | +2–4 px | over lowercase equivalent | tracking 0.18em–0.25em |

If text is wrapping awkwardly or you can only fit it by going below
these minimums, **change the layout, not the type size**.

## Layout heuristics

- **Three or more items in a row?** Verify each item still gets ≥320 px
  of horizontal space at 1080 wide. If not, stack vertically.
- **Cramped card grids → vertical stack** with title-left / body-right
  inside each row (see `IndustryDigitalTwin.tsx` for the canonical
  pattern).
- **Stat strips** stay horizontal (3 columns) but give each stat its own
  card with ≥24 px of inner padding so the value can breathe.
- **Footer pattern:** logo (or positioning line) on left, URL on right.
  Keep the URL ≥22 px. Avoid wrapping; prefer `whitespace-nowrap` + a
  shorter line over a smaller font.

## Component skeleton

Every post template MUST render its own background + content (the page
shell only provides the canvas frame and variant class):

```tsx
import { Logo } from '../components/Logo'
import bgSlides from '../../deck/assets/Background_slides.png' // light variant only

export const MyPost = () => (
  <>
    {/* Background image — MUST be a <div> with background-image, NOT an
        <img> with object-fit: cover. html2canvas does not honour
        object-fit reliably and the export will come out clipped. */}
    <div
      className="social-canvas__bg"
      aria-hidden="true"
      style={{ backgroundImage: `url(${bgSlides})` }}
    />

    {/* Optional readability overlay (div is fine — gradients export cleanly) */}
    <div className="social-canvas__overlay" style={{ background: '...' }} />

    {/* Padded content slot */}
    <div className="social-canvas__content text-hyve-header">
      {/* top: usually <Logo> + accent bar or category tag */}
      {/* middle — use flex-1 to push footer down */}
      {/* footer */}
    </div>
  </>
)
```

## Brand assets

- **Logos — MUST render via `<Logo>` component, NEVER as a raw `<img>`.**
  Both brand SVGs (`HD-Logo-dk2.svg`, `logo_white.svg`) define their
  fills via internal `<style>` + `class="cls-N"` blocks. html2canvas
  drops these when rasterising `<img>` tags, so paths fall back to
  black — the white wordmark disappears completely on dark posts. The
  `Logo` component (`src/pages/social/components/Logo.tsx`) fetches the
  SVG and inlines it into the DOM, which html2canvas handles correctly.
  Usage:
  ```tsx
  import { Logo } from '../components/Logo'
  // light variant:
  <Logo src="/HD-Logo-dk2.svg" alt="Hyve Dynamics" className="h-14 w-auto" />
  // dark / accent variant:
  <Logo src="/logo_white.svg" alt="Hyve Dynamics" className="h-14 w-auto opacity-90" />
  ```
- **Product image:** `/MatrixMesh-r5.png` (transparent PNG, ~260 px tall
  fits the corner of a 1080-wide canvas nicely). PNGs export fine as
  `<img>` — no Logo wrapper needed.
- **Backgrounds:** import from `src/pages/deck/assets/Background_slides.png`
  for light variant; dark/accent variants use the CSS gradient and
  optional radial overlays — do not add stock imagery
- **Tailwind brand tokens:** `text-hyve-header` (#2A303C), `text-hyve-text`
  (#3D4657), `text-hyve-accent` (#7FB3BE), `text-hyve-interactive-dark`
  (#028396). See `tailwind.config.js`.

## Adding a new template

1. Create `src/pages/social/posts/MyPost.tsx` with a JSDoc header
   containing: use case, suggested LinkedIn caption, variant note.
2. Import + append to `POSTS` in `SocialPostsPage.tsx`:
   ```tsx
   { id: 'my-post', label: 'My Post', variant: 'dark', Component: MyPost }
   ```
3. Swiper picks it up automatically — no other wiring needed.

## Exporting a post (1080×1350 PNG → Social_Posts folder)

The composer has a **Save PNG** button (bottom-right HUD). It:

- Forces `transform: scale(1)` on the active canvas so html2canvas captures
  at exact 1080 × 1350 px regardless of viewport zoom
- Awaits `document.fonts.ready` (fonts load asynchronously via main.tsx)
- POSTs the resulting `image/png` data URL to `/__save-post`
- The dev-only Vite plugin at `scripts/vite-plugin-save-posts.mjs` writes
  it to `SOCIAL_POSTS_OUTPUT_DIR` (configured in `vite.config.ts`,
  default `…/07_Brand_and_Web/Social_Posts`, override with the
  `SOCIAL_POSTS_DIR` env var)
- Filename pattern: `hyve-<post-id>-<ISO-timestamp>.png`

If you add CSS that html2canvas struggles with (advanced filters, certain
backdrop-blur cases, cross-origin images without CORS) the export may
fall back to a low-fidelity render. Test the Save PNG button after any
substantive styling change.

## Don'ts

- ❌ Don't change the 1080 × 1350 canvas size (breaks LinkedIn 4:5)
- ❌ Don't add inline emojis to the canvas unless the user asks
- ❌ Don't ship a poster that doesn't include `hyvedynamics.com` and a
  logo somewhere (usually the footer)
- ❌ Don't use type below 20 px for body content
- ❌ Don't add the social composer to production routes — it MUST stay
  behind `import.meta.env.DEV` checks in `src/App.tsx`
- ❌ Don't render brand SVGs as `<img>` — always use `<Logo>` (html2canvas
  drops internal `<style>` blocks)
- ❌ Don't use `<img>` for background images — always use a `<div>` with
  CSS `background-image` (html2canvas drops `object-fit: cover`)

## Tone (per Hyve brand)

Visionary & punchy — bold positioning, short lines, big claims always
backed by a concrete proof point (Tier 1 aerospace, 5 UK patents, ATI).
Canonical thesis line: **"The physical data layer for AI."**
Canonical positioning: **"The nervous system for machines in the AI era."**
Mirror the wording on `hyvedynamics.com` so posts and site reinforce
each other.
